  "narrative": "During the 1970s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in their identities and traditions. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of afro-textured hair, was a symbol of pride and self-acceptance. Cornrows, a traditional style originating from Africa, were also popular among Black and African women, often worn in simple or elaborate designs. As part of the protective hairstyles category, cornrows helped to reduce breakage and damage to Afro-textured hair. While dreadlocks and locs were not specifically mentioned as being popular during this decade, it is likely that they existed within some communities. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, but it is clear that Black and African women's hair culture was thriving in the 1970s, with a focus on embracing natural textures and protecting their hair from damage.",
